1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,770
Someone on the top floor of a skyscraper
actually ages faster than someone on the

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:04,480
ground floor!

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,390
Sounds like science fiction, doesn't it?

4
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:09,190
Two people live in the same building,
eat the same food,

5
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:11,390
and follow almost identical routines.

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:16,050
Yet, according to Einstein, their clocks
don't tick at exactly the same rate.

7
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,690
This happens because of gravity.

8
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:21,370
The stronger the gravitational field,
the slower time passes.

9
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:24,360
Since the top floor is slightly
farther from Earth's centre,

10
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:26,420
gravity is marginally weaker there.

11
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:29,580
So time passes just a tiny
bit faster at the top.

12
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:31,020
This isn't just a theory.

13
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:35,630
Scientists have used incredibly precise
atomic clocks to measure these tiny

14
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:37,920
differences in time at different heights.

15
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:40,510
Even a small change in
elevation can be measured.

16
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:43,390
Of course, the difference
is unbelievably small.

17
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:45,190
You won't notice it during your lifetime.

18
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,350
But technically, someone living
above you is ageing faster than you.

19
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:52,760
Would you move to the top floor if it
meant travelling slightly faster through

20
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:53,270
time?

